3:True Few-Shot Learning with Language Models
7:Abstract Pretrained language models (LMs) perform well on many tasks even when learning 1 from a few examples, but prior work uses many held-out examples to tune various 2 aspects of learning, such as hyperparameters, training objectives, and natural 3 language templates (“prompts”). Here, we evaluate the few-shot ability of LMs 4 when such held-out examples are unavailable, a setting we call true few-shot 5 learning . We test two model selection criteria, cross-validation and minimum 6 description length, for choosing LM prompts and hyperparameters in the true 7 few-shot setting. On average, both marginally outperform random selection and 8 greatly underperform selection based on held-out examples. Moreover, selection 9 criteria often prefer models that perform signiﬁcantly worse than randomly-selected 10 ones. We ﬁnd similar results even when taking into account our uncertainty in a 11 model’s true performance during selection, as well as when varying the amount 12 of computation and number of examples used for selection. Overall, our ﬁndings 13 suggest that prior work signiﬁcantly overestimated the true few-shot ability of LMs 14 given the difﬁculty of few-shot model selection. 15
17: e., few-shot learning [1–3]. Few-shot learning overcomes 18 many challenges with data-rich supervised learning: collecting labeled data is expensive, often 19 requires experts
21: and scales poorly with the number of tasks. However, the few-shot performance of 20 LMs is very sensitive to the textual task description [“prompt”; 3–6], order of training examples [6–8], 21 decoding strategy [9, 10], and other hyperparameters [3, 5, 9, 11, 12], as well as the learning algorithm 22 itself [3, 12]. Thus, effective model selection is crucial for obtaining good few-shot performance. 23 There are issues with how recent work approaches model selection in few-shot learning, however. 24 Prior work uses large train or held-out sets with many examples to choose prompts [2, 12, 13] and 25 hyperparameters [12]. Other work claims to use no validation set for hyperparameter selection [3, 26 11, 14] but does not describe how they design other aspects of their learning algorithm (e.g., training 27 objectives). It is unlikely that no validation examples were used, given the sophisticated nature of the 28 proposed algorithms. In this work, we examine if prior few-shot learning methods still perform well 29 when using only the provided examples for model selection, a setting we term true few-shot learning . 30 We ﬁnd that true few-shot model selection yields prompts that marginally outperform random selection 31 and greatly underperform selection based on held-out examples. Our result shows that prior work 32 may have greatly overestimated the few-shot ability of LMs. In other words, one reason that prompts 33 are so effective [“worth many examples”; 15] is that they are often tuned using many examples. We 34 evaluate two standard model selection criteria – cross-validation (CV) and minimum description 35 length (MDL) – ﬁnding that both obtain only limited improvements over random selection and 36 perform much worse than selection using held-out examples. For prompt selection, our observation 37 holds for 9 LMs ranging over 3 orders of magnitude in size [1, 2, 16] on 3 classiﬁcation tasks and 41 38 Submitted to 35th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2021). Do not distribute. tasks in the LAMA benchmark [17]. For choosing hyperparameters, true few-shot selection causes 39 performance to drop by 2-10% across 8 tasks for ADAPET [12], a state-of-the-art few-shot method. 40 Furthermore, true few-shot model selection has high variance in performance; selected models often 41 do much worse than randomly-chosen ones. We ﬁnd similar results when varying the number of 42 examples used, amount of computation, and conservativeness of our selection criterion. Altogether, 43 our results suggest that model selection is a fundamental roadblock to true few-shot learning. 44
23:2 Can We Do Model Selection in Few-Shot Learning?
25: 45 Prior work uses the phrase “few-shot learning” in multiple senses, raising questions about what it 46 means to do few-shot learning. We categorize few-shot learning into three distinct settings, each of 47 which assumes access to different data. Here, we formally disambiguate between these settings to 48 help future work avoid inadvertently comparing few-shot methods that operate in different settings. 49 Consider the supervised learning scenario where we have a dataset of inputs x 1: N and labels y 1: N , 50 sampled from a distribution over datasets D . We aim to determine the learning algorithm A ∗ ∈ 51 A 1 , . . . , A A with the smallest generalization loss L at predicting y given x on unseen validation 52 examples D val ∼ D after learning on training examples D train ∼ D . We say that an algorithm 53 A ( D train , R ) maps a training dataset D train and various random factors R that inﬂuence training to 54 a function that predicts y given x . A speciﬁes, e.g., the model architecture, hyperparameters, and 55 prompt. R includes random factors that impact the results of a learning algorithm, such as parameter 56 initialization and the order of training examples for online learning algorithms like stochastic gradient 57 descent. We say that A obtains a generalization loss L ( A ( D train , R ) , D val ) on a given validation set 58 D val . We aim to ﬁnd the A ∗ that minimizes the expected loss across training and validation sets: 59 In data-rich supervised learning , EL ( A , R ) is usually evaluated with a single train-validation split 60 ( D train , D val ) . Since large D train and D val are not always available, the traditional few-shot setting 61 evaluates EL ( A , R ) with many small ( D train , D val ) drawn from many, distinct distributions D [see, 62 e.g., work in meta-learning 18–21]. Each distribution D is sampled from D ∗ , a distribution over 63 distributions (e.g., of similar tasks), so we call this setting multi-distribution few-shot learning . 64 Recent work does not assume access to data from other distributions, performing few-shot learning 65 using only a few examples from a single distribution to update a pretrained LM [2, 12]. These papers 66 use a large validation set D val to tune the learning algorithm A , a setting we term tuned few-shot 67 learning . For example, Brown et al. [2] try prompts with different phrasings and numbers of training 68 examples to improve the validation accuracy of GPT-3. Tam et al. [12] choose the early stopping 69 iteration, prompt, and other model-speciﬁc hyperparameters based on validation performance. Tuned 70 few-shot learning relies on many labeled examples, so we argue that tuned few-shot learning does not 71 qualify as few-shot learning. If many validation examples are available, they could be incorporated 72 into the training set and trained on using data-rich supervised learning. Tuned few-shot learning 73 algorithms should be compared against data-rich supervised learning algorithms that use the same 74 amount of total data | D train | + | D val | . 75 In this work, we evaluate the success of tuned few-shot learning methods when no large D val is 76 available, a setting we term true few-shot learning . Formally, we aim to choose a learning algorithm 77 A with low expected loss EL ( A , R ) using only a small training set D train drawn from a single 78 distribution. Here, we must choose A by approximating EL ( A , R ) , e.g., using cross-validation. 79 Several papers claim to circumvent the need to estimate EL ( A , R ) by choosing hyperparameters 80 based on an educated guess [3, 9, 14]. However, the proposed learning algorithms themselves are 81 quite sophisticated, and it is unclear how they were designed if not by using validation performance. 82 Other work chooses the learning algorithm and hyperparameters using one or multiple other datasets 83 before evaluating on the target dataset [5, 11]. Such approaches fall under multi-distribution few-shot 84 learning and cannot be directly compared to methods that attempt to perform true few-shot learning, 85 even though prior work has made such comparisons [14]. 86 In what follows, we describe two model selection criteria – cross-validation and minimum description 87 length – which we use to evaluate tuned few-shot methods in the true few-shot setting. 88 2
29: 89 Cross-Validation (CV) [22–24] is one of the most widely used methods for estimating generalization 90 loss [25]. CV has also been used in prior work on multi-distribution few-shot learning [26, 27]. CV 91 randomly partitions D train into K equally-sized folds F ( D train ) 1 , . . . , F ( D train ) K and evaluates the 92 average loss on a validation fold F ( D train ) k when training on the remaining data F ( D train ) ¬ k : 93 In this way, CV forms K train-validation splits out of the pool of labeled examples. CV with one 94 example per fold ( K = N folds) is commonly referred to as leave-one-out CV (LOOCV). 95
37: 108 We evaluate the generalization loss of the algorithm chosen by CV (likewise for MDL): 109 where A CV = arg min A E R,F [ CV ( A , R, F )] . The above loss should be low in expectation, across different datasets D train ∼ D , D val ∼ D , 110 and random factors R, F : E D train ,D val ,R,F [ L ( A CV ( D train , R ) , D val )] . The loss should also be low 111 in variance: V D train ,D val ,R,F [ L ( A CV ( D train , R ) , D val )] . Low variance implies that CV/MDL reliably 112 choose an algorithm that generalizes to D val when trained with a given D train and random factors R, F . 113 Reliability is important for many practical or commercial applications where worst-case performance 114 is important, such as image recognition [35, 36], dialogue systems [37, 38], and robotics [39, 40]. 115 We also experiment with explicitly taking into account an algorithm’s variance during model selection, 116 choosing A CV to minimize a conservative estimate of CV, CV α ( A ) , chosen such that the probability 117 Pr R,F [ CV ( A , R, F ) < CV α ( A )] is high: 118 where α is a hyperparameter set based on the desired probability. In particular, if CV ( A , R, F ) 119 follows a normal distribution N when sampling R, F , then CV ( A , R, F ) ≤ CV α ( A ) with probability 120 (cid:82) α −∞ N ( µ = 0 , σ = 1) for a given R, F . CV α ( A ) resembles the Watanabe Akaike Information 121 Criterion [41], which estimates the generalization of a model trained with A using the expected loss 122 from a model trained with A plus the variance in training loss across models trained with A . 123 1 Online coding formally computes a sum over L ( . ) rather than the expectation, which differs by a constant factor. The two are equivalent for our purposes (ranking A ). 3
41: 124 Prior work has developed other model selection criteria such as the Akaike Information Criterion [AIC; 125 42], Watanabe-Akaike Information Criterion [WAIC; 41], and Mallows’ C p [43]. These methods 126 often rely on assumptions or quantities that are not available in the context of deep learning (AIC, 127 Mallows’ C p ) or are approximations of LOOCV (WAIC). Since state-of-the-art few-shot learning 128 methods tend to be based on deep learning, we focus on CV and MDL as our model selection criteria. 129 In Appendix §C, we also test several other criteria that are applicable to deep learning methods. 130 Selection criteria can be optimized automatically, e.g. with bayesian optimization [44–46], 131 evolutionary methods [45, 47, 48], reinforcement learning [49], or gradient descent [50–53]. Such 132 methods aim to match the performance of exhaustive search, the optimal approach (used in our work). 133
43:3 True Few-Shot Prompt Selection
45: 134 Recent work on LMs performs few-shot learning by providing training examples as input in the form 135 of a natural language “prompt” [2, 3, 9]. For example, for a question-answering task, Brown et al. [2] 136 prepend input examples with “READING COMPREHENSION ANSWER KEY” before providing 137 them to GPT-3 (see Appendix Table 2 for more examples). They then have the LM complete the 138 remaining words in the prompt, conditioning on earlier words (including various input examples), 139 following the LM’s pretraining objective (next word prediction). No parameter updates are involved. 140 It is not obvious a priori which prompts will generalize well for a given LM, and there is high variance 141 in how well different prompts generalize [3, 6], even between prompts with minor differences [e.g., 142 one comma; 5]. Effective prompt selection is thus crucial for true few-shot learning. 143
51:3.2 How well does prompt selection do in true few-shot learning?
53: 184 Fig. 1 (left) shows the results; prompt selection obtains marginal improvements over random selection 185 across model sizes ranging over 3 orders of magnitude. Prompts chosen by CV and MDL alike 186 underperform the best prompt (chosen using held-out performance) by 5-7% absolute on average. In 187 fact, prompts chosen based on held-out performance often outperform larger models whose prompts 188 are chosen in a true few-shot manner. CV and MDL do tend to choose better-than-average prompts, 189 but only close the gap between the average and best prompts by 20-40%, as shown in Fig. 1 (right). 190 We ﬁnd similar results for several other tasks (Appendix §B) and selection criteria (Appendix §C). 191 Fig. 2 (left) shows that CV/MDL struggle to choose the prompt with the highest test accuracy. Poor 192 top-prompt selection is especially prevalent for larger models like GPT-3 175B that have spurred 193 interest in prompt design (only 21% accuracy for CV vs. 9% for random chance). In Appendix §A, 194 we show that CV/MDL-chosen prompts differ from the best prompts not only in accuracy but also in 195 how effectively they transfer to models of different sizes. Altogether, our results show that optimal 196 prompt selection is difﬁcult in the true few-shot setting, and that prior work overestimated the ability 197 of LMs by using held-out examples for prompt selection. 198
57: If the average improvement from prompt selection is small, can we at least obtain an improvement 200 with high probability for a given task and train set? Fig. 1 (left) shows that the worst prompts do far 201 worse than average, so it would be useful if prompt selection helped to avoid the worst prompts. We 202 5 examine the probability with which prompt selection obtains various accuracy gains over the average 203 prompt and show results in Fig. 2 (middle) for CV (and similar results in Appendix §D for MDL). 204 CV/MDL-chosen prompts show high variance in test accuracy relative to the average prompt. For 205 most model sizes (.1B-6.7B), the chance of improving over the average, randomly-chosen prompt is 206 only ∼ 56% for CV and ∼ 55% for MDL. The performance of prompt selection forms a long-tailed 207 distribution; there is a ∼ 27% chance that prompt selection causes an accuracy drop of ∼ 13% for all 208 model sizes and CV/MDL alike. Furthermore, the tails grow heavier as model size increases. For 209 the largest model (GPT-3 175B), CV/MDL-chosen prompts sometimes do far worse than average, 210 e.g., 40% worse, 5% of the time. Our results suggest a troubling trend: as models grow bigger and 211 generalize better, our ability to reliably choose good prompts degrades. One possible explanation 212 is that larger models have the capacity to draw more complex decision boundaries, requiring more 213 examples to estimate the true expected loss on unseen examples; we may need to scale validation sets 214 along with model size. In Appendix §B, we show similar results for several other tasks. Overall, the 215 limited average-case gains from prompt selection cannot be expected with any reasonable conﬁdence 216 in the true few-shot setting, a problem that will only become worse with larger models. 217
65: The poor performance of prompt selection methods may be due to using such a small number of 229 labeled examples. As the number of labeled examples increases, we expect prompt selection methods 230 to improve. Thus, true few-shot prompt selection may be possible with a few dozen examples (though 231 it is not always possible to use more examples, due to limits on input length for LMs like GPT). We 232 therefore examine the test accuracy of CV/MDL-chosen prompts as we use an increasing number of 233 labeled examples N ∈ { 5 , 10 , 15 , 20 , 30 , 40 } . For N ≥ 10 , it is not feasible to marginalize over all 234 possible training example permutations, so we randomly sample 120 permutations (to match N = 5 ) 235 such that each example occurs the same number of times in each position (i.e., to use each example 236 as the held-out CV fold the same number of times). We run the experiment for ≤ 6.7B parameter 237 models, since it is prohibitively costly to run with larger models via the OpenAI API. 238 As shown in Fig. 3, there is no consistent trend in the performance of prompt selection, both in terms 239 of task performance (left) and in terms of accuracy at choosing the highest accuracy prompt (right). 240 6 Even in higher-data regimes (40 examples) CV/MDL struggle to choose effective prompts and do not 241 consistently, across model sizes, perform better than choosing examples based on 5 examples. Our 242 ﬁndings are surprising, because the true-few shot setting is where prompt design has been thought 243 most promising, due to the scarcity of training data [15]. However, the true few-shot setting is also 244 one in which prompt selection is hardest, greatly undermining the potential value of prompts. 245
69: In the preceding sections, we computed E R,F [ CV ( A , R, F )] using a ﬁxed number of samples for 247 R . Can we improve prompt selection by using more samples, at the cost of increased computation? 248 To answer this question, we vary the number of samples of R (and thus LM forward passes) used 249 to compute the above expectation and choose prompts as described in §2.3. To estimate CV with a 250 single forward pass, we sample a single fold k (here, a single example) and evaluate accuracy on fold 251 k when conditioning the LM on all others folds. Fig. 4 shows the results for N ∈ { 5 , 15 , 30 } training 252 examples using the largest model from §3.5 (GPT-3 6.7B). 253 Computation is not the bottleneck in prompt selection, as test accuracy roughly plateaus after one 254 forward pass for MDL and N forward passes for CV. This observation holds across N , as well as all 255 models with < 6.7B parameters (omitted for space). Our results suggest that true few-shot prompt 256 selection is fundamentally limited by the number of examples available. 257
71:4 True Few-Shot Hyperparameter Selection 258
73: Having shown that true few-shot prompt selection is difﬁcult, we now study the effectiveness of 259 model selection in the context of hyperparameter selection more generally. We examine the ADAPET 260 model [12], as it is open-source 3 and currently the top-performing few-shot model according to 261 SuperGLUE [61], a standard NLP benchmark. ADAPET ﬁnetunes the pretrained ALBERT xxlarge-v2 262 3 https://github.com/rrmenon10/ADAPET 7 LM [62] to (1) classify each label as correct/incorrect given the input and (2) to predict randomly 263 masked out input tokens given the label and unmasked input tokens, similar to Masked LM [63]. 264 ADAPET was developed in the context of tuned few-shot learning, as ADAPET’s hyperparameters 265 were chosen using validation examples. We examine how ADAPET does in the true few-shot setting. 266 We choose two hyperparameters in a true few-shot manner: the early stopping checkpoint and 267 fraction of words masked for the masked LM objective. ADAPET performs T = 1000 gradient 268 updates on batches of 16 examples and chooses the checkpoint at T ∈ { 250 , 500 , 750 , 1000 } 269 with the highest validation accuracy. ADAPET also chooses the best masking fraction M ∈ 270 { 0 . 075 , 0 . 10 , 0 . 105 , 0 . 15 } . Following ADAPET, we evaluate on SuperGLUE, a suite of 8 NLP 271 tasks. SuperGLUE consists of four question-answering tasks (BoolQ, COPA, MultiRC, ReCoRD), a 272 coreference resolution task (WSC), two entailment detection tasks (RTE, CV), and a commonsense 273 reasoning task (WiC); see Appendix §F.2 for task details. We use CV/MDL to choose T and M (out 274 of 16 total combinations) and then train a model on the full dataset with the chosen T and M . We use 275 FewGLUE [9], the 32-example subset of SuperGLUE used in prior work on few-shot learning. We 276 also use 3 other 32-example subsets that we randomly sample from SuperGLUE, to estimate variance 277 in performance across training sets. ADAPET uses a prompt during ﬁne-tuning, choosing the prompt 278 based on validation examples. To avoid using validation-tuned prompts, we use the ﬁrst prompt for 279 every task as the authors do for ablation studies. Since training ADAPET is expensive, we evaluate 280 CV/MDL with K = 8 folds. 4 We show results in Table 1. 281 Results Across all SuperGLUE tasks, CV/MDL hyperparameter selection performs similar to 282 or worse than average (randomly-chosen) hyperparameters and several points worse than the best 283 hyperparameters. In the true few-shot setting, the average SuperGLUE performance of ADAPET 284 drops below that of earlier methods (PET and iPET), highlighting how the use of validation examples 285 can give the false appearance of progress in few-shot learning. On MultiRC, CV/MDL choose 286 hyperparameters that give similar performance to the worst hyperparameters, another indication that 287 model selection methods do not consistently prevent worst-case behavior in the true few-shot setting. 288 Preliminary analysis in Appendix §E suggests that choosing better-than-average hyperparameters 289 requires several thousand examples. Overall, our results indicate that it is not just prompt selection 290 but model selection in general that is challenging in very low-data regimes. 291
77: 292 Our work shows that it is challenging to make even the most basic decisions about few-shot learning 293 algorithms using only a few labeled examples. Instead, it may be more promising to make additional 294 assumptions. The meta-learning setting assumes access to data from many other tasks in order to 295 perform learning and model selection [20, 21, 64, 65]. Transfer learning and multitask learning 296 assume access to data that is directly related to the task with limited data [66–69]. Data augmentation 297 techniques assume there is a viable way to create more data from limited data [70–73]. Other 298 approaches assume unlabeled data and develop unsupervised model selection techniques [74–76]. 299 4 See Appendix §F.4 for details on how we evaluate MDL on different SuperGLUE tasks. 8 When labeled data is cheap, the simplest approach is to assume more examples for validation— 300 in which case we might be better off training on the additional examples. Unless we make such 301 assumptions explicit, we cannot make meaningful comparisons between few-shot learning algorithms. 302 We ﬁnd the above avenues to be more promising future directions than true few-shot learning given 303 the challenge of model selection. 304 Inspired by prior work [77, 78], we offer recommendations for future work in true few-shot learning: 305 • Report all hyperparameters (prompts) considered and the hyperparameter selection criteria. 306 • Include validation examples in the number of examples used by a few-shot learning 307 algorithm. Validation examples include all examples used to decide on any aspect of learning: 308 hyperparameters, prompts, training objectives, decoding strategies, model architecture, etc. 309 • Once you have decided on the learning algorithm, submit your model for test evaluation 310 directly, without ﬁrst evaluating on validation. Report the total number of test evaluations 311 conducted (ideally, just one). Use the validation set only after test evaluation for any 312 ablations you report, to avoid making decisions about your algorithm with the validation set. 313 • Do not rely on hyperparameters from prior work that were tuned using validation examples 314 for the same benchmark (e . g., SuperGLUE), to avoid indirectly beneﬁting from validation 315 examples. Instead, re-tune such hyperparameters using only the given few-shot examples. 316 The above protocols are strict but mimic how a true few-shot learning algorithm would be used in a 317 real, low-data setting. To ensure researchers comply with such strict protocols, future benchmarks 318 may need to keep large test sets private while releasing only a few labeled examples. 319 Given our negative results on true few-shot learning, a major question remains: is it possible to 320 select models in a true zero-shot setting? Prior work uses LMs for zero-shot learning by choosing 321 an arbitrary prompt [17, 79] which requires no data but is suboptimal [4]. Other efforts try multiple 322 prompts and choose between them via trial and error alongside manual evaluation [1], effectively 323 leveraging human supervision. CLIP [13] achieves high zero-shot accuracy on ImageNet after 324 extensively tuning prompts and label names using ImageNet’s training set (1.28M examples), as we 325 noticed from the open-source code. 5 The authors report a 5% accuracy gain from tuning prompts 326 alone, but the training examples used for tuning are not available in true zero-shot learning. Without 327 any labeled data, the problem of model selection is even more challenging than in the true few-shot 328 case. Overall, our work provides guidance for future work in few-shot learning by clarifying the 329 assumptions made by the true few-shot setting and empirically demonstrates that model selection is a 330 major roadblock to true few-shot learning. 331
81: 332 We facilitate fair comparisons between few-shot methods in future work by disambiguating between 333 three few-shot settings: multi-distribution, tuned, and true few-shot learning. We highlight that 334 one setting, tuned few-shot learning, gives up the practical advantage of few-shot learning by using 335 many labeled examples. Furthermore, we show that several tuned few-shot learning algorithms work 336 signiﬁcantly worse in the true few-shot setting, without tuning on many examples. While we explored 337 CV, MDL, and several other criteria (Appendix §C), it is possible that effective true few-shot model 338 selection is possible using other criteria (§2.4) or heuristics not explored here. In this event, our work 339 will have discouraged work on a few-shot learning setting with applications to low-data settings, e.g., 340 that involve low-resource languages or expert annotation. Overall, however, we believe our work will 341 redirect future work to few-shot settings with more practical applications. 342 We show that it is hard to detect when a small input change hurts an LM’s generalization, even 343 when the change appears reasonable to human readers. We argue that practitioners will beneﬁt from 344 knowing such limitations, but they may also be discouraged from deploying LMs in many useful 345 contexts, such as question-answering, hate speech detection, automatic translation, and commercial 346 dialogue systems. Our ﬁndings may also encourage adversaries to target LM-based applications and 347 highlight which models are most susceptible to attack (e.g., larger models). By shedding light on the 348 shortcomings of (few-shot) LMs, we hope to spur future work to address these shortcomings. 349 5 https://github.com/openai/CLIP/blob/main/notebooks/Prompt_Engineering_for_ ImageNet.ipynb 9
85: 10 11 12 13 14 Checklist 586 1. For all authors... 587 (a) Do the main claims made in the abstract and introduction accurately reﬂect the paper’s 588 contributions and scope? [Yes] 589 (b) Did you describe the limitations of your work? [Yes] See §6. 590 (c) Did you discuss any potential negative societal impacts of your work? [Yes] See §6. 591 (d) Have you read the ethics review guidelines and ensured that your paper conforms to 592 them? [Yes] 593 2. If you are including theoretical results... 594 (a) Did you state the full set of assumptions of all theoretical results? [N/A] 595 (b) Did you include complete proofs of all theoretical results? [N/A] 596 3. If you ran experiments... 597 (a) Did you include the code, data, and instructions needed to reproduce the main 598 experimental results (either in the supplemental material or as a URL)? [Yes] Included 599 in the supplementary material. 600 (b) Did you specify all the training details (e . g., data splits, hyperparameters, how they 601 were chosen ) ? [Yes] We specify these details throughout our paper, since our work 602 aims to investigate the importance of such details. We describe our data splitting 603 procedure formally in §2, and dataset-speciﬁc details in §3 (especially §3.1) and §4. 604 (c) Did you report error bars ( e . g. , with respect to the random seed after running 605 experiments multiple times) ? [ Yes ] We show all plots and tables with error bars, 606 except where it is non-trivial to plot error bars (the heatmaps in Fig. 5) or when we 607 directly plot the distribution over errors (Fig. 2 middle/right). 608 (d) Did you include the total amount of compute and the type of resources used (e.g., type 609 of GPUs, internal cluster, or cloud provider)? [Yes] See Appendix §F.5. 610 4. If you are using existing assets (e . g., code, data, models) or curating/releasing new assets... 611 (a) If your work uses existing assets, did you cite the creators? [Yes] See §3.1, §B and 612 Appendix §F for citations for the dataset, prompts, and models we use for experiments 613 in §3. See §4 and Appendix §F for citations for the dataset and model we use for 614 experiments in §4. 615 (b) Did you mention the license of the assets? [Yes] See Appendix §F.3. 616 (c) Did you include any new assets either in the supplemental material or as a URL? [Yes] 617 We include the code to reproduce all experiments as supplementary material. 618 (d) Did you discuss whether and how consent was obtained from people whose data you’re 619 using/curating? [N/A] We only use publicly-released datasets that allow for use in a 620 research context. 621 (e) Did you discuss whether the data you are using/curating contains personally identiﬁable 622 information or offensive content? [Yes] See Appendix §F.3. 623 5. If you used crowdsourcing or conducted research with human subjects... 624 (a) Did you include the full text of instructions given to participants and screenshots, if 625 applicable? [N/A] 626 (b) Did you describe any potential participant risks, with links to Institutional Review 627 Board (IRB) approvals, if applicable? [N/A] 628 (c) Did you include the estimated hourly wage paid to participants and the total amount 629 spent on participant compensation? [N/A] 630 15
